          "text": "{\"relation\":\"REFINED\",\"consensus_summary\":\"Grapes primarily signify the fruitfulness, character, or covenant faithfulness of God’s people, with good and wild grapes marking righteousness and apostasy. Established secondary uses include blessing and abundance, Eucharistic blood, and people gathered and crushed in divine judgment.\",\"book_summary\":\"Grapes are persons understood as the ripened yield of a people’s works. Their pressing produces either covenant wine or the blood of judgment, depending on whether the vintage is good or wild.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Grapes are the moral or spiritual fruit produced by a covenant people, sometimes extending metonymically to the righteous or wicked people who embody that fruit.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"Grapes are persons in whom a people’s works have ripened, classified as good or wild and destined for covenantal wine or judgment.\",\"core_relation\":\"BOOK_NARROWS\",\"extension_relation\":\"PARTIAL_RECLASSIFICATION\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The book narrows generic fruitfulness, conduct, blessing, and abundance into a specific identification of grapes with persons formed as the mature yield of corporate works. It also systematizes pressing into two outcomes—covenant wine for a good vintage and judgment-blood for a wild one—rather than retaining all common grape associations, such as material abundance or grapes as a direct Eucharistic symbol.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"There is no direct contradiction: the consensus already recognizes grapes as covenant fruit and, in passages such as Micah 7 and Revelation 14, as representing righteous or judged persons. The material difference is that the book makes persons, rather than works or fruitfulness generally, the immediate referent and explains them as works ripened into personal form.\",\"rationale\":\"The book preserves the consensus’s central covenant-yield framework and its good-versus-corrupt and blessing-versus-judgment polarity. Its identification is nevertheless more restrictive and ontologically precise: grapes are not merely deeds, virtues, prosperity, or undifferentiated spiritual fruit, but persons embodying the mature result of a people’s works. This is a refinement rather than a novel or divergent replacement because personified covenant fruit is already recognizable within the consensus and its cited texts.\",\"citation_support\":\"PARTIAL\",\"strongest_objection\":\"The printed texts strongly support grapes as Israel’s moral yield, righteous or wicked persons, preserved servants, and objects of judgment, but they do not plainly state the book’s exact causal formula that a people’s works ‘ripen into persons.’ Nor do they clearly establish that every good pressing produces specifically covenant wine; that synthesis is inferred from the combined imagery.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
      ],
